[s0l0!-header][h1]The while Loop[/h1][/s0l0!-header] 

The [b]while [/b]statement is called a [b]loop structure[/b] because it executes statements repeatedly while an expression is true, looping over and over again. It takes the form: [code format="c" ]while (expression) {
  statements
}[/code]
The expression evaluates to either [b]true [/b]or [b]false[/b], and statements can be a single statement or, more commonly, a code block enclosed by curly braces { }.
[s0l0!-header][b]For example:[/b][/s0l0!-header][code format="c" codeId="1654"]#include <stdio.h>

int main() {
  int count = 1;
  
  [b]while [/b](count < 8) {
    printf("Count = %d\n", count);
    count++;
  }
    
  return 0;
}[/code]
The code above will output the [b]count [/b]variable 7 times.

The while loop evaluates a condition before the loop is entered, making it possible that the while statements never execute.
[note]An [b]infinite loop[/b] is one that continues indefinitely because the loop condition never evaluates to false. This may cause a run-time error. 
Run the code and see how it works![/note]

