[s0l0!-header][h1]The do-while Loop[/h1][/s0l0!-header] 

The [b]do-while[/b] loop executes the loop statements before evaluating the expression to determine if the loop should be repeated. 
It takes the form: [code format="c" ]do {
  statements
} while (expression);[/code]
The expression evaluates to either true or false, and statements can be a single statement or a code block enclosed by curly braces { }.
[s0l0!-header][b]For example:[/b][/s0l0!-header] [code format="c" codeId="1655"]#include <stdio.h>

int main() {
  int count = 1;
  
  [b]do [/b]{
    printf("Count = %d\n", count);
    count++;
  } [b]while [/b](count < 8);
    
  return 0;
}[/code]
Note the semicolon after the while statement.
The [b]do-while[/b] loop always executes at least once, even if the expression evaluates to false.
[note]Run the code and see how it works![/note]

