[s0l0!-header][h1]break and continue[/h1][/s0l0!-header] 

The [b]break [/b]statement was introduced for use in the [b]switch [/b]statement. It is also useful for immediately exiting a loop.
For example, the following program uses a [b]break [/b]to exit a [b]while [/b]loop:[code format="c" codeId="1656"]int num = 5;
  
while (num > 0) {
  if (num == 3)
    [b]break[/b];
  printf("%d\n", num);
  num--;
}[/code]
This program displays:
5
4
and then exits the loop.

When you want to remain in the loop, but skip ahead to the next iteration, you use the [b]continue[/b] statement. 
[s0l0!-header][b]For example:[/b][/s0l0!-header][code format="c" codeId="1657"]int num = 5;
  
while (num > 0) {
  num--;
  if (num == 3)
    [b]continue[/b];
      
  printf("%d\n", num);
}[/code]
The program output displays:
4
2
1
0
As you can see, the value 3 is skipped.

In the code above, if [b]num [/b]was decremented after the [b]continue [/b]statement an infinite loop would be created.
[note]Although the [b]break [/b]and [b]continue [/b]statements can be convenient, they should not be a substitute for a better algorithm.[/note]

